Ansible needs to connect to the remote server before it can perform any task.

For Linux servers, Ansible normally uses SSH for this connection.

There are two common ways to authenticate:

SSH Key Authentication
SSH Password Authentication

In this lab, we are using SSH Password Authentication.

1. How this lab works

We have:

WSL
(Ansible Controller)
       |
       | SSH + Username + Password
       ↓
AWS EC2
(Remote Server)

WSL is the Ansible controller because Ansible is installed there.

EC2 is the managed node because Ansible will perform tasks on it.

2. Why did we need the .pem key initially?

A new Ubuntu EC2 server normally allows SSH access using the AWS key pair.

So initially:

WSL
 |
 | .pem key
 ↓
EC2

We use the .pem file to get into the EC2 server for the initial configuration.

After logging in, we:

create a password for the ubuntu user
enable SSH password authentication
restart the SSH service

After that, we can connect using:

Username: ubuntu
Password: ********

So the .pem key is mainly used for the initial setup in this lab.

3. Creating a password

Ubuntu EC2 doesn't normally have a password configured for SSH login.

We create one using:

sudo passwd ubuntu

This sets a password for the ubuntu user.

4. Enabling password authentication

SSH controls how users can authenticate through its configuration.

The configuration is:

/etc/ssh/sshd_config

But in our EC2 instance, we found another configuration file:

/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60-cloudimg-settings.conf

It contained:

PasswordAuthentication no

This means:

SSH will not allow users to log in using a password.

We changed it to:

PasswordAuthentication yes

This means:

SSH is now allowed to authenticate users using a password.

5. Restarting SSH

After changing the SSH configuration, we restart the SSH service:

sudo systemctl restart ssh

This makes SSH load the new configuration.

We can verify the configuration with:

sudo sshd -T | grep -i passwordauthentication

Expected:

passwordauthentication yes

6. Testing SSH manually

Before using Ansible, we should first make sure normal SSH password authentication works.

From WSL:

ssh ubuntu@EC2_IP

For example:

ssh ubuntu@15.207.55.228

SSH asks:

ubuntu@15.207.55.228's password:

If the password is correct, we enter the EC2 server.

This is important because Ansible also uses SSH.

If normal SSH doesn't work, Ansible won't work either.
